WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:03.040
. Let's get right into audience questions,
first one today.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.650
David, I've heard you say that the stock market
does better with Democratic presidents than

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:07.980
Republican.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:14.130
Does the country always do better when there
is a Democratic president economically?

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:19.600
So we have to know what metrics we're looking
at when we try to measure how the country

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.919
is doing.

00:00:20.919 --> 00:00:23.710
There are a number of different indicators
that we would look at.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:26.060
No one indicator tells us everything we need
to know.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.630
We would look at GDP growth, unemployment
and stock market performance.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:35.000
Certainly as indicators, we would probably
take a look at what portion of the country

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.899
is poor below the poverty line.

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:41.239
What's the degree of inequality?

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:45.610
There are other metrics that can be a little
squishier because they relate to others.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.800
So we could say, well, how how strong is the
dollar?

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.829
But that would be related to our trade deficit
or surplus.

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:59.120
And it's harder to say we're doing well or
we're not without kind of a more systemic

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.120
approach.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:01.579
And then you've got intangibles, right?

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:05.820
Like how respected are we around the world
right now?

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.740
Respect for the United States is very, very
low because of Donald Trump.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.100
How do you sort of price that into the equation?

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:19.150
But to pick a few of these indicators, yes,
the stock market does better under Democratic

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:21.070
presidents than Republicans.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:22.880
A lot of people don't care about the stock
market.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.370
The stock market is not the economy, as we
have very clearly seen during the last three

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:27.549
months of corona virus.

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:33.590
But the return, the annualized return during
Democratic administrations has been fourteen

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:38.340
point three percent, whereas it has been ten
point eight percent under Republicans.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:42.659
So, yes, the stock market does better under
Democrats in terms of GDP growth.

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:49.609
GDP growth is significantly higher under Democratic
presidents than Republican presidents.

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:56.780
Typically, it depends on exactly how you measure
and the uncertain timing issues.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:02:04.789
But it's anywhere from 15 to 44 percent larger
GDP growth during Democratic presidential

00:02:04.789 --> 00:02:09.360
administrations than Republican in terms of
unemployment.

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.560
Unemployment tends to be higher during Republican
administrations, 18 percent higher by one

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:18.310
study that I looked at in terms of job creation.

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:22.170
Remember jobs created and the unemployment
rate or do two different things.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:29.800
Since World War Two, Democratic presidents
have seen more than twenty four point four

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:35.170
million more jobs created during their watch.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:42.560
That's an average of 78 percent more jobs
created per year of Democratic administrations

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.350
versus Republican administrations.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:50.730
So, yeah, by those indicators, we do better
with Democratic presidents.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:56.200
Now, the thing is, somebody believes Republicans
are just better for the economy.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.780
These data won't convince them.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.650
They'll say, well, some of that is because
of lag time.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:07.490
You see better metrics when a Democrat takes
over because of the policies the Republicans

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.490
put in place.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:12.010
This has been pretty widely debunked, but
still people continue to repeat it.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.310
You will sometimes hear, well, Democrats

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:19.160
spend in order to make some of these metrics
look better.

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:24.520
Well, it's like if you trust the metrics and
government spending improves the metrics,

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:25.520
then that's good, right?

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.370
I mean, they were the right will try to argue
that these are sort of like cheap ways to,

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:32.740
quote, improve the economy, which sounds crazy.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:34.690
I mean, it literally sounds crazy.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:35.760
Other metrics like poverty.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:45.390
I mean, the thing about poverty is if I change
the poverty level, if I say, well, instead

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:51.360
of being poor at fifteen thousand dollars
a year, you're only poor below twelve.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:53.830
I've just taken millions of people out of
poverty.

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:55.760
I've not really changed anything.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:00.400
So that's why poverty is not really the best
metric.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:05.300
Inequality is important, but as always, you
know, mean median mode.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:10.070
Looking at something like the Guinea Index
JNI, there's different ways to look at that.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:16.959
But in short, yeah, I mean, the traditional
indicators that we look at suggest that the

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:23.180
economy does better when we have a Democratic
president than a Republican president since

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:29.169
the early nineteen hundreds, when we have,
you know, more or less or really even the

00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:34.810
World War two era, we would say when things
are sort of measured in the same way.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.520
And you can always mean

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:42.970
the reason I'm sort of hesitating is that
this question is inherently fraught because

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:48.910
any empirical differences you can point to
can be explained away by motivated reasoning.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:55.620
And so it's a fair question, but it's a really
difficult one to convince anybody about in

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.759
a way that would materially change their perspectives.

00:04:58.759 --> 00:04:59.759
On what?

00:04:59.759 --> 00:04:59.763
The right policies are.

